     1	# Phase 6.4-3 — Python worker + Arrow exchange + debugpy · DESIGN
     2	
     3	**Status:** ✅ DESIGNED 2026-05-28. Ready to implement in a fresh session (multi-cycle — the
     4	6.4 entry plan estimates 2-3 sessions for this sub-increment alone).
     5	**Predecessor:** 6.4-2 trait wiring + napi DTO surface FULLY SHIPPED (engine HEAD `a9992a32e67`).
     6	The dispatch substrate is in place: `FunctionRegistry::udf_handles: HashMap<String,
     7	FunctionImplHandle>` + `udf_handle(name)` reader (6.4-2 cycle 1), `register_function` /
     8	`unregister_function` / `list_functions` over napi, `fn_gen` cache invalidation (6.4-1 H3),
     9	`ArgContext` binder migration (6.4-1 H1). **Missing:** the actual dispatch — there is no
    10	`RegisteredFn::Udf` variant; `scalar.rs:463` returns `#NAME?` for any name without a dispatch
    11	entry, including registered UDFs.
    12	
    13	**Mandate:** Decision-lock §2 item 6 — the wedge. A user registers a Python function; it
    14	participates correctly in the graph (deps, volatility, dirty fanout — all already wired by
    15	6.4-0/6.4-1/6.4-2); 6.4-3 makes it actually *compute* by routing the call to an out-of-process
    16	Python worker and returning the result as an Arrow batch.
    17	
    18	## 0. Current ground truth (verified at source 2026-05-28)
    19	
    20	- `crates/quantbook-py/` exists but is a **20-line stub** (`src/lib.rs` + a stub
    21	  `python/quantbook/__init__.py`), NOT a workspace member, referenced by nothing. Greenfield.
    22	- `RegisteredFn` (`ql-functions/src/registry.rs:172`) is a tagged-union dispatch enum:
    23	  `Scalar` / `RangeAware` / `ContextAware` / `Unified` / `ReferenceAware`. **No `Udf` variant.**
    24	- Eval dispatch is a `match` on `RegisteredFn` in `ql-exec/src/scalar.rs:165-463`; the `None`
    25	  arm (`:463`) returns `Value::Error(ErrorValue::Name)` (#NAME?). A registered-but-undispatchable
    26	  UDF currently hits this arm (proven by the 6.4-2 `register_function_dirties_dependent_formulas`
    27	  test: `MYUDF(A1)` stays #NAME? after registration).
    28	- `FunctionReturn` = `Scalar(Value) | Array(ArrayValue)` (`registry.rs`); the cell-boundary spill
    29	  path already consumes `Array`. A UDF return maps onto this same enum.
    30	- `FunctionImplHandle(u64)` is opaque (`ql-session`); 6.4-2 stores it per-UDF in `udf_handles`.
    31	
    32	## 1. The central design decision — sync recalc vs out-of-process call
    33	
    34	The engine's recalc is **synchronous**: scalar eval returns a `Value` inline; `recompute_*`
    35	walks the graph topologically and evaluates each dirty node. An out-of-process Python call is
    36	inherently blocking I/O. Two models:
    37	
    38	### Model A — inline blocking call (per-cell, timeout-guarded) — RECOMMENDED for v1
    39	At the `RegisteredFn::Udf` dispatch arm, marshal args → send one request to the worker →
    40	**block the recalc thread** on the response (with a deadline) → map the Arrow batch back to
    41	`FunctionReturn`. Worker-kill on timeout/cancel.
    42	- **Pros:** fits the existing sync eval path with zero changes to the recompute scheduler;
    43	  smallest blast radius; correct dep/dirty semantics are already wired upstream.
    44	- **Cons:** one IPC round-trip per UDF cell (N cells calling the UDF = N round-trips). Arrow
    45	  framing amortizes the *payload* cost but not the round-trip count. Acceptable for v1 (the wedge
    46	  is "does it work + debug correctly," not "10⁶ UDF cells").
    47	- **Mitigation path (defer):** the `BatchShape::ArrayBatch` metadata axis (6.4-1) exists precisely
    48	  so a future increment can switch array-shaped UDFs to a **batched** pass (collect all calls to
    49	  one UDF in a recompute pass → one Arrow batch → one round-trip). Design the protocol so this is
    50	  additive (a `call_batch` frame alongside `call`).
    51	
    52	### Model B — batched pass (collect-then-dispatch) — DEFER to 6.4.x
    53	A pre-pass collects every dirty UDF call into per-function Arrow batches, dispatches them, then
    54	the eval pass reads cached results. Correct and fast but requires a new recompute phase + a
    55	results cache keyed by (node, fn_gen). Too invasive for the wedge.
    56	
    57	**Decision: Model A for 6.4-3.** Design the wire protocol batch-ready (see §3) so Model B is a
    58	later, additive optimization, not a rewrite.
    59	
    60	## 2. Process & crate architecture
    61	
    62	```
    63	ql-exec (eval)  ──RegisteredFn::Udf(handle)──▶  WorkerHandle (in ql-exec or a new ql-udf crate)
    64	                                                      │ length-prefixed Arrow IPC over a duplex pipe
    65	                                                      ▼
    66	                                          Python worker process (spawned, managed)
    67	                                                      │ imports user module in a trusted workspace
    68	                                                      ▼
    69	                                          quantbook (Python pkg): register_formula_function,
    70	                                                      qb.show / qb.publish / qb.bind
    71	```
    72	
    73	- **New crate `ql-udf`** (Rust): owns the `WorkerHandle` — spawn / handshake / request-response /
    74	  timeout / kill / drain. Pure process+IPC mechanics; depends on `arrow` + `ql-types` (+ a small
    75	  protocol module). NOT on `ql-exec` (leaf, dependency-inversion like `ql-io-csv`/`ql-io-xlsx`).
    76	  `ql-exec` depends on `ql-udf` and injects a `WorkerHandle` into the eval context.
    77	  - *Alternative:* put the worker handle directly in `ql-exec`. Rejected — keeps `ql-exec` from
    78	    growing a subprocess+IPC concern and matches the established leaf-crate + inversion pattern.
    79	- **`crates/quantbook-py`** (the existing stub): the worker entrypoint (a `python -m quantbook.worker`
    80	  loop) + the user-facing `quantbook` Python package (`register_formula_function`, `qb.*`). The
    81	  Rust `src/lib.rs` may host a PyO3 bridge later; for v1 the worker is a **plain Python process**
    82	  speaking the Arrow-IPC protocol over stdio — no PyO3 in the hot path (simpler, debugpy-friendly,
    83	  no GIL-in-Rust complications). Promote `quantbook-py` to a workspace member.
    84	- **`FunctionImplHandle(u64)`** indexes a worker-side registry of user functions (the handle is
    85	  minted IDE-side when the user calls `register_formula_function`, passed to `registerFunction`
    86	  over napi, stored in `udf_handles`, and echoed in each dispatch request so the worker knows which
    87	  Python callable to invoke).
    88	
    89	## 3. Wire protocol (length-prefixed Arrow IPC over a duplex pipe)
    90	
    91	Framing: `[u32 LE length][u8 frame-type][payload]`. Frame types:
    92	- `HELLO` / `HELLO_ACK` — handshake: protocol version, worker pid (for debugpy), capability flags.
    93	- `CALL { handle: u64, call_id: u64, args: Arrow RecordBatch }` — one UDF invocation. Args encoded
    94	  as an Arrow batch (scalars = 1-row; ranges/columns = N-row; the `ArgContext`/`BatchShape`
    95	  metadata determines the schema). `call_id` correlates the response + supports cancel.
    96	- `RETURN { call_id, result: Arrow RecordBatch }` — success; maps to `FunctionReturn::Scalar`
    97	  (1×1) or `Array` (N×M).
    98	- `RAISE { call_id, exc_type, message, traceback }` — Python raised; maps to a deterministic
    99	  `CellDiagnostic` + the cell value `#CALC!`/`#VALUE!` per contract (exit test 7).
   100	- `CANCEL { call_id }` — cooperative cancel request (best-effort; hard cancel is process kill).
   101	- `LOG { level, message }` — worker stdout/stderr surfaced as diagnostics (never silently dropped).
   102	
   103	**Arrow ↔ engine `Value` mapping** (the fiddly part — its own cycle):
   104	- engine `Value::{Number, Text, Boolean, Error, Blank}` ↔ Arrow `Float64 / Utf8 / Boolean / (error
   105	  as a sentinel union or a separate error column) / null`. `ArrayValue` ↔ a 2-D batch (row-major).
   106	  Error values need an explicit encoding (Arrow has no native spreadsheet-error type) — a tagged
   107	  struct column `{ kind: Utf8 ("number"|"text"|"bool"|"error"|"blank"), num, str, bool, err }` is
   108	  the safe v1 encoding (verbose but unambiguous; optimize later).
   109	
   110	## 4. Eval-path wiring (`RegisteredFn::Udf`)
   111	
   112	1. Add `RegisteredFn::Udf(FunctionImplHandle)` to the enum (`registry.rs:172`).
   113	2. `register_udf` (6.4-2) currently stores metadata + handle but registers NO `RegisteredFn`.
   114	   6.4-3: also insert `RegisteredFn::Udf(handle)` into the dispatch table (`fns`) so the
   115	   `scalar.rs` match finds it instead of falling to the `None` #NAME? arm. (Re-examine the
   116	   6.4-2 atomicity invariant — `register_udf` becomes a 3-way atomic: metadata + handle + dispatch.)
   117	3. New match arm in `scalar.rs` (~before `:463`): `Some(RegisteredFn::Udf(handle)) => { … }` —
   118	   marshal the evaluated args to an Arrow batch, call `ctx.worker.call(handle, batch, deadline)`,
   119	   map `RETURN`→`FunctionReturn`, `RAISE`→error value + diagnostic, timeout→worker-kill + `#CALC!`.
   120	4. The `FunctionContext` / `EvalContext` gains access to the `WorkerHandle` (a new optional field;
   121	   `None` when no worker is configured → registered UDF with no worker = deterministic
   122	   `#CALC!`/diagnostic, NOT a panic — No-Fallbacks-honest).
   123	
   124	## 5. Cancellation / worker-kill (contract §6.2 — hard cancel)
   125	
   126	- `CancelPolicy::WorkerKill` (already a `FunctionMetadata` axis, 6.4-1) → cancel/timeout kills the
   127	  worker process. The engine must survive a killed worker: the `WorkerHandle` respawns lazily on
   128	  the next call; in-flight `call_id`s resolve to a deterministic cancel diagnostic.
   129	- Exit test 6: a canceled/timed-out UDF must NOT commit a late result — `call_id` correlation
   130	  drops any `RETURN` arriving after the deadline/kill.
   131	- FaultGuard discipline (6.1C M3 / 6.4-2): a worker round-trip happening inside recompute must not,
   132	  on worker death, leave the session torn. The worker call is I/O at the leaf; failures map to a
   133	  cell error, not an engine fault.
   134	
   135	## 6. debugpy
   136	
   137	- Worker spawned with an env/flag that, in trusted-workspace mode, starts `debugpy` listening on a
   138	  port (surfaced in `HELLO_ACK` → IDE can attach). Off by default; opt-in per the trusted-workspace
   139	  gate. No effect on the protocol hot path.
   140	
   141	## 7. Security (trusted-workspace gating — exit tests)
   142	
   143	- The worker imports + executes user Python. This is **trusted-workspace only** (the IDE gates it;
   144	  the engine never auto-spawns a worker for an untrusted workbook). Exit tests: trusted-workspace
   145	  gating, no untrusted-Python file write, worker-kill leaves the engine usable.
   146	
   147	## 8. Python user API (`quantbook` package)
   148	
   149	- `register_formula_function(fn, *, name, volatility=…, …)` → returns/registers a handle; the IDE
   150	  bridges this to `Session.registerFunction(metadata, implHandle)` over napi.
   151	- `qb.show` / `qb.publish` / `qb.bind` — the authoring surface (the basic `register_formula_function`
   152	  flow is the 6.4-3 core; `qb.show`-only rendering is deferred to 6.4.5 per the entry plan §4).
   153	
   154	## 9. Cycle decomposition (each its own audit; 6.4-3 spans multiple sessions)
   155	
   156	- **6.4-3a — protocol + `ql-udf` crate (Rust-only, no Python):** framing, frame types, the
   157	  Arrow↔`Value` codec with round-trip property tests, `WorkerHandle` against a **mock in-process
   158	  responder** (no subprocess yet). Audit: 2-way. *This is the clean cycle-1 sliver.*
   159	- **6.4-3b — real Python worker + spawn/handshake/kill:** `quantbook-py` worker loop, process
   160	  lifecycle, timeout→kill, respawn. Audit: 2-way (engine) + smoke against a real `python`.
   161	- **6.4-3c — eval wiring (`RegisteredFn::Udf` + scalar.rs arm + EvalContext worker):** end-to-end
   162	  `=MYUDF(A1)` computes. Audit: 3-way (engine + Opus + IDE, per the cross-repo lesson).
   163	- **6.4-3d — debugpy + trusted-workspace gating + IDE bridge** (cross-repo). Audit: 3-way.
   164	- Then **6.4-4** exit-tests + closure megaudit (5-way).
   165	
   166	## 10. Open questions to resolve at implementation start
   167	
   168	1. **Transport:** stdio duplex vs a unix-domain socket / named pipe. Stdio is simplest + debugpy
   169	   coexists (debugpy uses its own port); a socket decouples logging from the protocol. Lean stdio
   170	   for v1 with stderr reserved for LOG passthrough.
   171	2. **`python` discovery:** which interpreter? (workspace venv vs system). Trusted-workspace config
   172	   provides the path; fail loud if absent (No-Fallbacks).
   173	3. **Arrow dependency:** `arrow` crate version + feature surface; keep `ql-udf` lean.
   174	4. **Re-examine 6.4-2 `register_udf` atomicity** once it also inserts a `RegisteredFn::Udf`
   175	   dispatch entry (3-way atomic; the cycle-2 audit's atomicity invariant must extend).
   176	5. **`FunctionContext` worker access** — threading a `&WorkerHandle` through the eval context
   177	   touches every dispatch arm's signature; scope the churn (likely an `Option<&WorkerHandle>` on
   178	   `EvalContext`).
   179	
   180	## 11. Reading list (start-of-implementation)
   181	1. This doc + `docs/phase6/6-4-entry-plan.md` §3.6.4-3/§3.6.4-4.
   182	2. `docs/api/session-api.md` §6.2 (cancellation) + §10.4 (UDF exit tests 1-8).
   183	3. `crates/ql-functions/src/registry.rs` (`RegisteredFn`, `FunctionReturn`, `udf_handle`,
   184	   `register_udf`).
   185	4. `crates/ql-exec/src/scalar.rs:165-463` (the dispatch match — where the `Udf` arm goes).
   186	5. `crates/ql-io-csv` / `ql-io-xlsx` (the leaf-crate + dependency-inversion pattern for `ql-udf`).

